<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minamoto no Yoritomo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1147">1147</a>–1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Yoritomo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minamoto no Yoritomo was the founder and first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate. Leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan">Minamoto clan</a> to victory over the Taira in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180–1185, he set up a warrior government at Kamakura, appointed military governors and stewards across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and destroyed the Northern Fujiwara in 1189. He received the title of shogun in 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo.jpg" alt="Minamoto no Yoritomo" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Takanobu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minamoto_no_Yoritomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoritomo was the third son of Minamoto no Yoshitomo and Yura Gozen, daughter of Fujiwara no Suenori, High Priest of Atsuta Shrine. He was born at the family villa beside Atsuta Shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, and was a descendant of Emperor Seiwa. His childhood name was Onitakemaru. In the Hōgen Rebellion of 1156 his father sided with the winning party of Emperor Go-Shirakawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> and had his own father executed.</p><p>In December 1159 Yoshitomo joined Fujiwara no Nobuyori in the Heiji rebellion, attacked Sanjō Palace and seized the emperor and the retired emperor while Kiyomori was away. Kiyomori returned and defeated the Minamoto at Rokuhara, and Yoshitomo was betrayed and executed. Yoritomo and his younger brothers were spared because of their age and sent to temples as monks. He grew up in exile and married Hōjō Masako, daughter of Hōjō Tokimasa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1180 Prince Mochihito called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan">Minamoto clan</a> throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to arms against the Taira, and Yoritomo took part. He set up his base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> in the east. His uncle Minamoto no Yukiie and his cousin Minamoto no Yoshinaka plotted against him. He lost his first major battle, at Ishibashiyama in September 1180, and spent the next six months raising a new army. His brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoshitsune">Minamoto no Yoshitsune</a> and Minamoto no Noriyori defeated the Taira in several battles, though Yoshinaka entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1183. From 1181 to 1184 a truce with the Taira-dominated court gave Yoritomo time to build an administration of his own at Kamakura.</p><p>In the summer of 1189 he invaded the northern provinces of Mutsu and Dewa and razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a>, the capital of the northern Fujiwara. Go-Shirakawa died in spring 1192, and Yoritomo took the title of Sei-i Tai Shōgun, which formally placed the feudal lords, the jitō and the shugo under his control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> was left with national ceremony and ritual.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the great hunt at Fuji in May 1193 the brothers Soga Sukenari and Soga Tokimune killed Kudō Suketsune, the killer of their father, and Tokimune then raided Yoritomo's mansion before being stopped. Yoritomo was ordained a Buddhist monk in 1199 and died two days later, aged 51.</p><p>After his death his wife's family, the Hōjō, held power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> as regents, under the title of shikken, until 1333. The historian George Bailey Sansom wrote that Yoritomo was a truly great man, with foresight and practical good sense in building machinery to match his growing power. A stone pagoda beside Shirahata Shrine is traditionally believed to be his grave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minamoto no Yoritomo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1180 to 1185 the Genpei War between the Taira and Minamoto clans ended with the victory of <b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> and the founding of the Kamakura shogunate, later dominated by the Hōjō clan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> defeated the Taira and in victory seized power from the civil aristocracy, reducing the emperor and his court to symbolic figureheads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside Europe, historians have drawn the closest parallel with Japan, where <b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> became shogun in 1192 and rewarded his vassals, the gokenin, with offices and rights over land.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In a night attack near the Fuji River, in present-day Shizuoka Prefecture, Minamoto forces of some 30,000 men under <b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> routed the Taira army of Taira no Koremori, who escaped.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death his son Yasuhira killed Yoshitsune under pressure from <b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> in 1189, but Yoritomo invaded anyway, and the family was destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-fujiwara">Northern Fujiwara</a></li><li><blockquote>The Genpei War of 1180 to 1185 brought <b>Minamoto no Yoritomo</b> to power and the Kamakura shogunate, though the imperial court survived in Kyoto.</blockquote><a href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Atsuta-ku, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.13,136.91&amp;year=1180" aria-label="Atsuta-ku on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8018 893 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Minamoto no Yoritomo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8468 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8468 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.13,136.91&amp;year=1180">What was here in 1180? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
